# Battleships game


# Overview

A two-player Battleships game simulation implemented in C, featuring ship placement, turn-based attacks, statistical analysis, and matrix-based encryption and decryption.

The program supports multiple matches, tracks player performance, and processes statistical queries, including encrypted requests and responses.

# Implementation

The system is divided into three main components:

Game Simulation: Manages ship placement, board representation, player attacks, and win detection.

Statistical Analysis: Evaluates player accuracy and determines how different outcomes could improve overall performance.

Encryption & Decryption: Uses matrix operations and modular arithmetic to decrypt statistical requests and encrypt their results.

# Game Mechanics

Each match involves two players with separate game boards.

The game follows these steps:
Initializes the boards and reads ship configurations.
Validates ship positions and places valid ships on the boards.
Displays both game boards.
Processes alternating attacks, identifying missed shots, successful hits, and destroyed ships.
Determines the winner when the required victory condition is reached.

The program also tracks successful and total attacks to calculate player accuracy.

# Statistical Analysis

The system implements four types of statistical queries to analyze player performance.

These operations evaluate how changes to match results could affect a player's accuracy, including scenarios involving individual matches and overall performance across multiple matches.

Sorting algorithms are used to identify which match results have the greatest impact on the overall statistics.

# Encryption & Decryption

Statistical requests can be provided in encrypted form.

The encryption and decryption mechanism uses:
Matrix multiplication and transformations
Determinant calculation
Adjugate matrices
Modular multiplicative inverses
Modular arithmetic

Encrypted requests are decrypted before processing, and their corresponding results are encrypted before being displayed. When encryption is disabled, statistical queries are processed directly.

# Memory Management

The implementation uses dynamic memory allocation for one-dimensional and two-dimensional arrays, including game boards and intermediate matrices.

Allocated memory is released when it is no longer needed or at the end of execution.

# Input and Output

The program reads input from standard input (stdin) and prints results to standard output (stdout).

The input consists of:
Number of matches
Game board dimensions
Ship configurations for both players
Player attack coordinates
Encryption key and statistical queries
The program outputs game boards, attack results, match winners, and statistical query results.

# Build and Run

Requirements:
GCC compiler
Make
C99 support

Compilation:
make build

Execution:
./battleships

Alternatively, the program can read input from a file:
./battleships < input.txt

To save the output to a file:
./battleships < input.txt > output.txt

Cleanup:
make clean